Introduction

Python provides the datetime module to work with dates and times. It provides classes and methods for creating, formatting, parsing, and performing calculations with dates and times.

1. Importing the datetime Module
import datetime

The module contains useful classes such as:

date – works with dates
time – works with time
datetime – works with both date and time
timedelta – performs date and time calculations
2. Working with Dates

The date class is used to represent dates.

Getting Current Date
from datetime import date

today = date.today()
print(today)
Creating a Specific Date
specific_date = date(2024, 2, 16)
print(specific_date)
Extracting Date Components
print(today.year)
print(today.month)
print(today.day)
year → Year
month → Month
day → Day
Finding the Day of the Week
print(today.weekday())
print(today.isoweekday())
weekday() → Monday = 0, Sunday = 6
isoweekday() → Monday = 1, Sunday = 7
3. Working with Time

The time class is used to represent a specific time.

from datetime import time

specific_time = time(14, 30, 15)

print(specific_time)
print(specific_time.hour)
print(specific_time.minute)
print(specific_time.second)
hour → Hour
minute → Minute
second → Second
4. Working with Date and Time Together

The datetime class handles both date and time.

Getting Current Date and Time
from datetime import datetime

now = datetime.now()
print(now)
Creating a Specific Date and Time
specific_datetime = datetime(2024, 2, 16, 14, 30, 15)
print(specific_datetime)
Extracting Components
print(now.year)
print(now.month)
print(now.day)
print(now.hour)
print(now.minute)
print(now.second)
5. Formatting Date and Time

The strftime() method converts a date/time into a formatted string.

formatted_date = now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")
formatted_time = now.strftime("%H:%M:%S")
formatted_datetime = now.strftime("%d-%b-%Y %I:%M %p")

print(formatted_date)
print(formatted_time)
print(formatted_datetime)
Common Formatting Codes
Code	Meaning
%Y	Full year
%m	Month
%d	Day
%H	Hour (24-hour format)
%I	Hour (12-hour format)
%M	Minutes
%S	Seconds
%p	AM/PM
%A	Full weekday name
%B	Full month name
6. Parsing Strings into Dates

The strptime() method converts a string into a datetime object.

date_string = "16-02-2024 14:30"

parsed_date = datetime.strptime(
    date_string, "%d-%m-%Y %H:%M"
)

print(parsed_date)
Important Difference
strftime() → Date/Time → String
strptime() → String → Date/Time
7. Date and Time Arithmetic

The timedelta class is used to perform calculations with dates and times.

Adding Days
from datetime import timedelta

future_date = today + timedelta(days=7)
print(future_date)
Subtracting Days
past_date = today - timedelta(days=3)
print(past_date)
Adding Hours
future_time = now + timedelta(hours=2)
print(future_time)

timedelta can be used with values such as:

Days
Hours
Minutes
Seconds
Weeks
Quick Summary
Class / Method	Purpose
date	Work with dates
time	Work with time
datetime	Work with date and time
timedelta	Date/time calculations
date.today()	Get current date
datetime.now()	Get current date and time
strftime()	Format date/time as string
strptime()	Convert string to date/time
weekday()	Get weekday number
isoweekday()	Get ISO weekday number
Key Takeaway

Python's datetime module makes it easy to create, access, format, parse, and perform calculations on dates and times.